        "cost": 0.01,
        "tokens": {
          "input": 1200,
          "output": 320,
          "reasoning": 0,
          "cache": {
            "read": 0,
            "write": 0
          }
        }
      },
      "parts": [
        {
          "id": "prt_msg_ses_35_08_01",
          "sessionID": "ses_35",
          "messageID": "msg_ses_35_08",
          "type": "step-start"
        },
        {
          "id": "prt_msg_ses_35_08_02",
          "sessionID": "ses_35",
          "messageID": "msg_ses_35_08",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "If everything the route talks to is mocked, that's really a unit test of the route handler, not an integration test — the whole point of an integration test is to exercise several real pieces together (route → controller → real test database, say) so you catch problems in how they connect, which full mocking can't do. For this one, I'd use a real test database and let the route/controller talk to it for real, and only mock things that are genuinely external to the system, like a third-party email API if one were involved."
        },
        {
          "id": "prt_msg_ses_35_08_03",
          "sessionID": "ses_35",
          "messageID": "msg_ses_35_08",
          "type": "step-finish",
